    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111118\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n418 AM PDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Well above normal temperatures continue Friday, then temperatures \n   drop 5-10 degrees this weekend. \n\n*  Isolated convection possible in Northern Mojave and Lincoln \n   Counties Friday. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the\n   region Friday through the weekend. With reduced humidities \n   leading to fire weather concerns.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nWhile Thursday will likely have been the hottest day of the week, \nwell above normal temperatures continue Friday before temperatures \ncool down this weekend by 5-10 degrees. A hot airmass is currently \nin place across the region with 850mb temperature anomalies well \nabove normal for this time of year, supporting highs at or above 100 \nin desert valleys and in the CRV. The strong ridge which built in \noverhead earlier in the week is showing signs of breaking down as a \ntrough pushes into the PNW. By Friday, the 500mb high shifts \nsoutheast into AZ, then continues eastward through the weekend \nbecoming centered over Southern New Mexico while the PNW trough \npushes inland. Influence from the incoming trough, lowering upper \nheights, and strong subsidence shifting out of the region should all \nresult in cooling surface temperatures across the Mojave Desert and \nGreat Basin. Low and mid level temperature anomalies still remain \nabove normal for this time of year, so still expect daily highs and \nlows above normal as well...but not at the levels seen this past \nweek. Lingering monsoonal moisture will also help keep nighttime \nlows elevated the next few days with lows in the 80s likely in \ndesert valleys, preventing much relief from the heat. By next week, \na persistent troughing pattern looks to set up over the west coast, \nkeeping southwesterly flow aloft locally and preventing strong \nridging from developing overhead. Medium to long range guidance \nkeeps low and mid level temperature anomalies and upper level height \nanomalies near to slightly above normal for this time of year into \nnext week. This raises confidence that temperatures will continue to \nrun above normal by a few degrees for both highs and lows into the \nextended period; however with Heat Risk remaining low to moderate, \nnot expecting dangerous heat conditions to return in this pattern. \nHighs generally in the 90s with lows in the 70s can be expected in \ndesert valleys, the CRV, and death valley in the extended while \ncooler temperatures are likely at higher elevations. \n\nAs mentioned above, lingering monsoonal moisture still remains in \nplace for portions of the region just ahead of the trough. Hi-res \nguidance shows the potential for scattered convection in portions of \nLincoln and Mohave counties Friday as a weak disturbance passes \nthrough. Added chance wording in the forecast to account for this \nthreat, but not expecting much impacts from anything that does \ndevelop. Brief periods of heavy rain, lightning, and gusty winds \npossible with any storm that does develop. \n\nThe aforementioned trough pushing into the western US will also \nincrease southwest winds across the region. Guidance continues to \nshow a 40 kt mid level jet streak developing overhead Saturday into \nSunday. Mixing into this area of stronger winds will result in \nwidespread gusts mixing down to the surface 30-35 mph. There could \nalso be some gusty southerly winds impacting Lake Mohave and Lake \nHavasu this weekend, which could impact boaters with winds and \nwaves. Since the low to mid level jet ramps up during the overnight \nhours Friday and Saturday night, the strongest winds in the region \nmay occur in wind prone areas of San Bernardino county with the \nnightly westerly push and also at higher elevations across the \nregion. Looking at today's Ensembles and NBM probs, all guidance has \nkept the threat relatively low for gusts over 40mph. While it does \nstill look breezy with frequent gusts over 30mph, latest trends \nlower confidence in frequent gusts over 40 mph and the possibility \nof needing a wind advisory. Still will monitor forecast trends \nthrough the week and will update the forecast accordingly. \n\nThis coupled with very dry conditions has increase fire weather \nconcerns, with a Fire Weather Watch in effect for Nye, Esmeralda, \nand portions of Lincoln and Clark counties for Saturday. Winds look \nto reduce for Sunday but remain breezy. Longer term models are \nstarting to favor breezy winds peaking around 25 mph each afternoon \ninto the first half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast \nPackage...Southerly winds develop late this morning and increase \nto over 10 knots. These winds will strengthen further in the \nafternoon and take a southwesterly direction, with gusts generally\nbetween 20 and 25 knots. Winds decrease slightly but remain gusty\novernight. FEW to SCT mid and high clouds will filter through the\narea. Temperatures exceed 100 degrees between 19Z and 02Z. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds ramp up this afternoon areawide. Peak gusts will \nbe in the 20 to 25 knot range for most locations. Stronger winds are \nexpected at KDAG, with gusts between 25 and 30 knots through most of \nthe day and between 30 and 35 knots overnight. The onset of westerly \nwinds will be in the late afternoon at KBIH as northerly to \nnorthwesterly winds persist through most of the day. FEW to SCT mid \nand high clouds will filter through the area. Isolated thunderstorms \nmay develop this afternoon over parts of Mohave and Lincoln \ncounties, but these should not impact any terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
